(* ==========================================================================
   Butter VM — Instruction Set Architecture
   A stack-based virtual machine that is the compilation target for the
   Butter AST defined in GRAMMAR.bnf. Implemented in src/value.zig,
   src/chunk.zig, src/vm.zig, and src/compiler.zig.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. DESIGN GOALS ("RISC applied to a stack machine")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* A classic RISC ISA gets its simplicity from three commitments: every
   instruction is the same width and decodes the same way, instructions
   are load/store only (arithmetic never touches memory directly, only
   registers), and the instruction set is small and orthogonal (one job
   per instruction, compose the rest in software). This VM applies the
   same three commitments to a stack machine instead of a register machine:

   a) FIXED-WIDTH INSTRUCTIONS. Every instruction is the same
      `struct { op: OpCode, operand: u32 }` (see src/chunk.zig), regardless
      of what `op` is. There is no variable-length encoding and no
      instruction that carries more than one operand. Unused operands are
      simply zero. This trades code density (a real byte-packed encoding
      would be smaller) for a trivial, uniform decode step — appropriate
      for a teaching/target VM where correctness and simplicity dominate.
      LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX (section 3) need two compile-time constants
      (an array's base slot and its length) but keep to this same one-u32
      shape by packing both into the one operand (16 bits each) rather
      than growing Instruction a second field — see `chunk.packIndexOperand`.

   b) LOAD/STORE ONLY. Arithmetic, comparison, and logical instructions
      (ADD, LT, NOT, ...) take zero operands and work purely on values
      already on the stack; the only instructions that name a location
      (a constant-pool slot or a local-variable slot) are the LOAD/STORE
      family (PUSH_CONST, LOAD_LOCAL, STORE_LOCAL). Nothing else reaches
      into the constant pool or a variable slot.

      The two byte-buffer I/O instructions (READ and WRITE_BYTES, section
      9) are the one deliberate exception: they read and write a whole
      run of slots directly. That is unavoidable for I/O — the entire
      point is to move bytes between a stream and storage the program
      already declared — but note they still don't *name* those slots
      themselves: they're handed an ARRAY_REF on the stack, produced by
      the ordinary LOAD/STORE-family instructions, so the "which memory"
      decision stays where this goal wants it.

   c) ORTHOGONAL AND MINIMAL. There is exactly one conditional-branch
      instruction (JUMP_IF_FALSE) and one unconditional one (JUMP); a
      JUMP_IF_TRUE was deliberately omitted because 'or' is expressible as
      JUMP_IF_FALSE + JUMP (see section 4), the same way real RISC ISAs
      favor a minimal set of branch primitives that software sequences
      into everything else.

   Now that the source language has functions (GRAMMAR.bnf, design notes
   3b-d), the VM has a call stack: every function's LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL
   operands are slot indices *relative to the current call frame's base
   pointer*, not absolute (see sections 5 and 6). Top-level code is simply
   frame 0, with its base pointer fixed at 0 for the program's whole
   lifetime — which is why nothing about existing top-level compilation
   changed when functions were added.

   There is also no type-checking pass: the compiler resolves variable
   *names* to slots at compile time but does not check that a variable's
   declared type agrees with what's stored into it, and the VM checks
   *runtime* value types operation-by-operation (section 3). Static type
   checking would be a separate semantic-analysis pass layered on top of
   the existing parser output; it isn't attempted here. *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. VALUE REPRESENTATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

<value>    ::= INT | FLOAT | BOOL | STRING | ARRAY_REF | STREAM | NULL | OBJECT

(* INT and FLOAT are i64 / f64. STRING values are borrowed slices into a
   Chunk's constant pool (which in turn borrows from the original source
   text) — never separately allocated, copied, or freed at run time.
   Because GRAMMAR.bnf defines no string-concatenation operator and no
   growable/heap-allocated collections, every string a running program can
   ever produce already exists as a literal fixed at compile time. That
   means the VM needs no heap and no garbage collector: every `Value` is
   either a plain scalar, a slice it never owns, or (ARRAY_REF) a
   reference to storage that lives elsewhere on this same value stack —
   see the addendum below and section 6's generic-array addendum. *)

(* ARRAY_REF (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3j) is `{ base: u32, len: u32 }` —
   `base` an ABSOLUTE index into the VM's `stack` array (not frame-relative:
   it's computed once, at MAKE_ARRAY_REF time, from whichever frame's `bp`
   the referenced array actually lives in — section 6), `len` its length.
   It is the runtime representation of a GENERIC (unsized, `int[]`-style)
   array parameter or return value ONLY; a fixed-size array (design note
   3e) never produces one — see the addendum right below this one for why
   those two remain deliberately different runtime representations. There
   is no way to construct an ARRAY_REF from Butter source directly (no
   syntax makes one appear as a literal); the only way one comes into
   being is MAKE_ARRAY_REF, always compiler-generated where a fixed-size
   local has to be handed over by reference — at a call site passing it
   into a generic parameter (`compiler.zig`'s
   `compileGenericArrayArgument`), or as the buffer of a READ/WRITE_BYTES
   (section 9). Both routes go through the same `compiler.zig` helper,
   `emitArrayRef`, which is exactly why the I/O instructions need no
   fixed-vs-generic variants of their own. *)

(* STREAM (GRAMMAR.bnf design notes 3k and 3l) is `value.Stream`, a tagged
   union: `{ .standard: Standard }` naming one of the three fixed keywords
   stdin/stdout/stderr, or `{ .file: u32 }` naming a slot in the VM's own
   open-file table (section 10) — never a raw OS file handle or
   descriptor. There is no way to construct a `.file` stream from Butter
   source except through OPEN (section 10); a literal `stdin`/`stdout`/
   `stderr` keyword use instead compiles to a PUSH_CONST of a `.standard`
   stream constant, exactly like any other literal. A stream is a small,
   trivially-copyable value with no ownership semantics of its own — it
   never needs to be freed, and copying one (assigning it to a variable,
   passing it as an argument) copies only which stream it names, never any
   underlying resource — which is what keeps the VM's "no heap, no
   allocator at run time" property intact even though `open`'s result is
   now a genuine runtime value. *)

(* NULL (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3n) is JSON's `null` — `value.Value`'s
   `null_value` variant, carrying no payload. There is exactly one null
   value; copying or discarding it is as free as any other scalar.

   OBJECT (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3m) is `*value.Object`, a refcounted
   heap allocation — the VM's first and only heap-managed value, and the
   one genuine exception to every "no heap, no allocator at run time" claim
   elsewhere in this document. See section 11 for its representation and
   the refcounting rules that manage its lifetime. *)

(* A compiled program is one main Chunk (the top-level code, run as an
   implicit frame 0 — see section 6) plus one Chunk per declared function,
   each with its OWN constant pool. Nothing is shared between them:
   per GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3b, a function body can only ever reference
   its own parameters/locals, never a top-level variable or another
   function's locals, so there is no reason for their constant pools or
   local-slot numbering to overlap or coordinate. *)

(* Arrays (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) are NOT a `<value>` variant — there
   is no runtime "array value" that gets pushed, popped, or passed around
   as a single unit. An array is simply `<declared size>` consecutive
   stack slots (section 5), and every individual slot still holds one
   plain scalar `<value>` exactly as before. This is what lets arrays fit
   into a VM with no heap and no garbage collector: the array itself isn't
   a new kind of runtime object, just a compile-time-known grouping of the
   same slots locals always lived in. *)

(* `len(arr)` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3i) on a FIXED-size local compiles
   straight to PUSH_CONST of the array's declared size — the compiler
   already has that number in hand (it's `Local.array`'s `.fixed` payload
   — section 5), so `len` needs no dedicated opcode there and does nothing
   at runtime beyond what any other INT literal does; the constant folding
   happens entirely at compile time. On a GENERIC local (design note 3j),
   there IS no compile-time number to fold to — the actual length depends
   on whatever array the caller passed — so `len(arr)` instead compiles to
   LOAD_REF_LEN, a genuine runtime read of the reference's own `len` field
   (section 3). *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. INSTRUCTION SET
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* Stack effect notation: ( before -- after ), stack grows to the right. *)

   PUSH_CONST   #idx     ( -- v )            push constants[idx]
   PUSH_TRUE              ( -- true )
   PUSH_FALSE             ( -- false )
   POP                    ( v -- )            discard the top of stack

   LOAD_LOCAL   #slot     ( -- v )            push stack[slot]
   STORE_LOCAL  #slot     ( v -- v )          stack[slot] = v (peeks, does
                                               not consume — this is what
                                               lets 'x := 5' be used as an
                                               expression, per GRAMMAR.bnf's
                                               <assignment> production)

   LOAD_INDEX   #packed(slot,length)  ( index -- v )
                                               pops index (must be INT);
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise.
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= index < length. Else
                                               pushes stack[slot + index]
                                               (slot/length both unpacked
                                               from the one operand — see
                                               section 1a).
   STORE_INDEX  #packed(slot,length)  ( index v -- v )
                                               pops v, then index (same
                                               Type/BoundsError checks as
                                               LOAD_INDEX), sets
                                               stack[slot + index] = v,
                                               then pushes v back — the
                                               same peek-not-consume shape
                                               as STORE_LOCAL, so
                                               'arr[i] := v' is usable as
                                               an expression too. The
                                               index must be compiled
                                               (and therefore pushed)
                                               BEFORE the value, which is
                                               why the stack effect reads
                                               index-then-v rather than
                                               v-then-index.

   MAKE_ARRAY_REF #packed(slot,length)  ( -- ref )
                                               pushes a fresh
                                               ARRAY_REF{ base = bp + slot,
                                               len = length } (slot/length
                                               both compile-time constants,
                                               unpacked the same way
                                               LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX's
                                               operand is). Synthesizes a
                                               reference to a FIXED-size
                                               local at `slot` — GRAMMAR.bnf
                                               design note 3j — only ever
                                               emitted when passing such a
                                               local into a generic (`[]`)
                                               parameter.
   LOAD_INDEX_REF #slot   ( index -- v )      pops index (must be INT);
                                               reads ref = stack[bp+slot]
                                               (must be ARRAY_REF —
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise);
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= index < ref.len; else
                                               pushes stack[ref.base+index].
                                               The generic-array counterpart
                                               to LOAD_INDEX: `slot` names
                                               where the REFERENCE lives,
                                               not the array itself — its
                                               base/length come from the
                                               reference at runtime instead
                                               of the operand.
   STORE_INDEX_REF #slot  ( index v -- v )    generic-array counterpart to
                                               STORE_INDEX, same reference
                                               indirection and peek-not-
                                               consume shape as
                                               LOAD_INDEX_REF/STORE_INDEX.
   LOAD_REF_LEN  #slot    ( -- int )          reads ref = stack[bp+slot]
                                               (must be ARRAY_REF); pushes
                                               ref.len as an INT. What
                                               `len(arr)` compiles to for a
                                               GENERIC array, since (unlike
                                               a fixed-size one) its length
                                               isn't a compile-time constant
                                               to fold to.

   ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV, MOD, POW
                          ( a b -- r )        both operands must be numeric
                                               (INT or FLOAT); INT op INT
                                               stays INT (overflow-checked);
                                               any FLOAT operand promotes
                                               both to FLOAT. DIV/MOD by
                                               zero and INT overflow are
                                               runtime errors, not traps.

   NEG                    ( a -- r )          numeric only
   NOT                    ( a -- r )          BOOL only

   EQ, NEQ                ( a b -- bool )     defined for every pair of
                                               value types: numeric operands
                                               compare across INT/FLOAT by
                                               value; any other mismatched
                                               pair of types simply compares
                                               unequal (this mirrors '=='
                                               in most dynamically-checked
                                               languages, rather than being
                                               a type error)
   LT, LTE, GT, GTE        ( a b -- bool )    numeric operands only

   JUMP         #target               ip := target
   JUMP_IF_FALSE #target  ( bool -- bool )    peeks (does not pop) the
                                               condition; if false, ip :=
                                               target. Every use site is
                                               responsible for POPping the
                                               condition itself on both the
                                               taken and fall-through paths
                                               (section 4) — this is the one
                                               instruction in the ISA with a
                                               "conditional" stack effect,
                                               and confining that to a single
                                               well-tested opcode is the
                                               payoff for keeping everything
                                               else unconditional.
                                               TypeMismatch if the peeked
                                               value is not BOOL — this is
                                               the sole place 'if'/'while'
                                               conditions and 'and'/'or'
                                               operands are checked to
                                               actually be booleans.

   CALL         #func_index  ( arg1 ... argN -- result )
                                               the N arguments (N = the
                                               callee's arity) are already
                                               on the stack, pushed by
                                               ordinary expression codegen
                                               before CALL executes; CALL
                                               itself pushes nothing. It
                                               suspends the current chunk
                                               (saving its ip and base
                                               pointer as a new frame — see
                                               section 6) and jumps to
                                               functions[func_index]'s own
                                               chunk at instruction 0, with
                                               the base pointer set so the
                                               callee's LOAD_LOCAL/
                                               STORE_LOCAL slot 0 is exactly
                                               where arg1 already sits.
                                               CallStackOverflow if the
                                               frame stack is already at
                                               capacity.
   RET                     ( v -- v )          pops the current frame
                                               (discarding every argument
                                               and local it owns — see
                                               section 6), then leaves v,
                                               the value the returning
                                               function computed, as the
                                               sole result on the caller's
                                               stack. Every function chunk
                                               is guaranteed by the
                                               compiler to end in a RET
                                               (see GRAMMAR.bnf design note
                                               3c) — this is the only way a
                                               function chunk's execution
                                               ever ends.

   PRINT                  ( v -- )            pop v, write it followed by
                                               a newline, always to the
                                               `out` stream (section 9)

   READ         #stream   ( ref -- count )    pops an ARRAY_REF naming the
                                               destination buffer
                                               (TypeMismatch if it isn't
                                               one), reads at least 1 and
                                               at most ref.len bytes from
                                               `stream`, stores each as an
                                               INT in 0..255 into
                                               stack[ref.base + i], and
                                               pushes how many were read.
                                               Pushes 0, and only 0, at end
                                               of input; slots past `count`
                                               are left untouched. A SHORT
                                               read, like POSIX read(2) —
                                               see section 9 for why.
                                               StreamReadFailed if the
                                               underlying stream errors.
   WRITE        #stream   ( v -- count )      pops v, writes it to `stream`
                                               in exactly PRINT's rendering
                                               but with NO trailing
                                               newline, and pushes the
                                               number of bytes that took.
                                               StreamWriteFailed if the
                                               underlying stream errors.
   WRITE_BYTES  #stream   ( ref count -- written )
                                               pops count (must be INT),
                                               then an ARRAY_REF; the exact
                                               inverse of READ. Bounds- and
                                               range-checked:
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= count <= ref.len, and
                                               ByteOutOfRange if any of the
                                               first `count` elements isn't
                                               an INT in 0..255 (TypeMismatch
                                               if one isn't an INT at all).
                                               Writes those bytes to
                                               `stream` and pushes how many
                                               were written. The count is
                                               pushed AFTER the reference,
                                               which is why the stack effect
                                               reads ref-then-count.

   MAKE_LIST    #n        ( v1..vn -- list )  moves the top n values into a
                                               fresh list Object (a pure
                                               move — neither the moved
                                               values nor the fresh list
                                               need an incref). #0 is what a
                                               bare `list xs` (no
                                               initializer), or a
                                               `list`-returning function
                                               falling off the end, compiles
                                               to. See section 11.
   MAKE_MAP     #n        ( k1 v1 .. kn vn -- map )
                                               builds a fresh map Object
                                               from n key/value pairs, in
                                               order (a repeated key is
                                               last-wins). TypeMismatch if a
                                               key isn't string-shaped
                                               (defense in depth — the
                                               compiler only ever emits a
                                               string-literal key here).
                                               See section 11.
   INDEX_GET               ( container index -- v )
                                               pops index, then container;
                                               dispatches on container's
                                               kind (list: index must be
                                               INT, bounds-checked; map:
                                               index must be string-shaped,
                                               KeyNotFound if absent; else
                                               TypeMismatch). Pushes the
                                               found value, increffed. What
                                               bracket-indexing compiles to
                                               whenever the indexed thing
                                               isn't a bare local resolving
                                               to a fixed/generic array
                                               (section 11).
   INDEX_SET               ( container index v -- v )
                                               same dispatch as INDEX_GET,
                                               mutating instead: a map
                                               insert-or-updates (never
                                               errors on a missing key); a
                                               list requires an in-bounds
                                               index. Decrefs whatever
                                               value it replaces. Peek-
                                               shaped like STORE_INDEX: v is
                                               still on the stack afterward.
   LIST_PUSH               ( list v -- newlen )
                                               appends v to list (a pure
                                               move) and pushes its new
                                               length. TypeMismatch if the
                                               first operand isn't a list.
   MAP_HAS                 ( map key -- bool ) whether key is present, with
                                               no error for a missing one
                                               (unlike INDEX_GET).
   MAP_DELETE               ( map key -- bool )
                                               removes key if present
                                               (freeing its owned key copy,
                                               decreffing its value);
                                               pushes whether it existed.
   MAP_KEYS                ( map -- list )     a fresh list of fresh
                                               heap-string COPIES of map's
                                               own keys (never borrowed from
                                               the map's own storage), in
                                               insertion order.
   LEN_VALUE                ( v -- int )       what the relaxed
                                               `len(<expression>)` compiles
                                               to whenever the argument
                                               isn't a bare fixed/generic-
                                               array identifier: list ->
                                               element count, map -> entry
                                               count, else TypeMismatch.
   JSON_PARSE               ( ref count -- v )  gathers `count` bytes from
                                               the buffer ref points at
                                               (reusing WRITE_BYTES's own
                                               `gatherBytes`/`io_buffer`
                                               staging area unchanged) and
                                               parses them as JSON, pushing
                                               whatever the document's root
                                               turns out to be.
                                               JsonParseFailed (with a
                                               `Vm.diagnostic`) if the bytes
                                               aren't well-formed JSON. See
                                               section 12.
   JSON_STRINGIFY           ( v -- string )    renders v as JSON text and
                                               pushes it as a fresh heap
                                               `string`. TypeMismatch if v
                                               is (or contains, nested
                                               inside a list/map) a stream
                                               or an array reference —
                                               neither has a JSON
                                               representation. See
                                               section 13.

   HALT                                       stop execution (only ever
                                               appears in the main chunk,
                                               at the very end — see
                                               section 6)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. CODEGEN PATTERNS (how the compiler lowers control flow and short
      circuiting onto the instructions above — the standard "backpatched
      jump" technique)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   if-stmt (condition, then, optional else):

       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       POP                          (* discard a truthy condition *)
       <then-branch>
       JUMP  end_target
     else_target:
       POP                          (* discard a falsy condition — this pop
                                        happens whether or not there is an
                                        else-branch, which is what keeps the
                                        stack balanced either way *)
       [ <else-branch> ]
     end_target:

   while-stmt:

     loop_start:
       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP

   'and' (short-circuits to a false left operand without evaluating right):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  end_target    (* left false -> skip right, its false
                                        value is the result *)
       POP                          (* left true -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:

   'or' (short-circuits to a true left operand without evaluating right;
   built from JUMP + JUMP_IF_FALSE rather than a dedicated JUMP_IF_TRUE,
   per the minimality goal in section 1c):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       JUMP  end_target             (* left true -> skip right, its true
                                        value is the result *)
     else_target:
       POP                          (* left false -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. VARIABLE RESOLUTION (compile time, no runtime name lookup)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   The compiler keeps a list of (name, scope_depth, slot, width) entries
   in declaration order, separately per chunk being compiled (the main
   chunk, and then each function's chunk in turn — see section 6). `slot`
   is tracked explicitly (a running `next_slot` counter) rather than
   implied by position in that list, because `width` isn't always 1: a
   plain scalar local occupies exactly one slot, but an array local
   (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) occupies `<declared size>` consecutive
   slots starting at `slot`, so slot numbers and list position diverge as
   soon as any array is in scope. Entering a <block> increases
   scope_depth; leaving it removes every local declared at that depth
   from the compiler's list, decrements `next_slot` by each removed
   local's width (so a later sibling scope reuses the same slots instead
   of growing the frame unboundedly), and emits one POP per slot removed
   (an array-local's whole width, not just one POP) so the runtime stack
   shrinks back in step with the compile-time model. A reference to an
   undeclared name, or an assignment to one, is a compile error
   (`CompileError.UndefinedVariable`) — one of the few forms of semantic
   checking this compiler does perform, since it is required just to
   assign slots at all, not an optional extra. Shadowing an outer variable
   in an inner block is allowed (lookup walks the locals list from the
   end, so the innermost matching declaration wins); redeclaring the same
   name twice *within one scope* is not rejected — it simply shadows the
   earlier slot from that point on. This mirrors the Lox-family "locals
   resolved to stack slots" design (as in Crafting Interpreters' clox).

   An array-typed local's bare name resolves for LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL
   purposes exactly like a scalar's (same lookup, same list) but using it
   that way is a compile error (`CompileError.ArrayUsedAsScalar`) — a
   FIXED-size array is only ever accessed through LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX
   (section 3), which pack that same `slot` together with `width` (there
   called `length`, for the bounds check) into one instruction operand.

   `Local.array` (compiler.zig) distinguishes which of two array kinds a
   local is, or `null` for a plain scalar: `.fixed(n)` (width `n`,
   accessed via LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX as above — GRAMMAR.bnf design note
   3e) or `.generic` (width exactly 1 — it's a single ARRAY_REF value, not
   raw slots — accessed via LOAD_INDEX_REF/STORE_INDEX_REF/LOAD_REF_LEN
   instead, design note 3j). The bare-name-as-scalar restriction above
   applies to BOTH kinds identically; a generic local's reference value
   still can't be loaded/stored as a plain expression outside the narrow
   contexts section 6's generic-array addendum describes (forwarding it as
   a call argument or a return).

   A function's parameters are simply its first locals, occupying slots
   0..arity-1, added to that function's locals list before its body is
   compiled at all — from the compiler's perspective a parameter is just a
   local that already has a value by the time the body starts, the same
   way a var-declaration's initializer already has a value on the stack
   before the declaration finishes (section 2). A scalar parameter costs
   one slot; a `.fixed(n)` array-typed parameter costs `n` consecutive
   slots, exactly like a fixed-size array-typed local anywhere else (see
   the array-typed-local paragraph just above); a `.generic` array-typed
   parameter costs exactly one slot too (it's a single ARRAY_REF value,
   design note 3j) — which is precisely why `arity` (the total slot width
   CALL reserves, section 6) and the parameter *count* (what the
   arity-mismatch check in GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3d actually compares)
   are two different numbers once any parameter is fixed-size-array-typed. Locals lists are entirely separate per chunk: a
   function's locals list starts fresh (containing only its own
   parameters) and is discarded once that function's chunk is done
   compiling, which is what makes it structurally impossible for a
   function body to resolve a top-level variable's name — the compiler
   never even has that name in scope while compiling the function (per
   GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3b).


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. FUNCTION CALLS & RECURSION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   Each declared function compiles to its own `chunk_mod.Function` — a
   name, an arity, a return_width, and a self-contained Chunk (own
   instructions, own constant pool). A compiled `Program` is one main
   Chunk (top-level code) plus a slice of these, indexed by the operand
   CALL instructions use to name their target (resolved by name to that
   index at compile time — see compiler.zig's function table, built before
   any function body is compiled so that calls resolve regardless of
   declaration order, including a function calling itself or two functions
   calling each other).

   ARITY AND RETURN_WIDTH ARE STACK-SLOT COUNTS, NOT VALUE COUNTS. `arity`
   is the total number of stack slots every argument together occupies —
   a scalar argument is 1 slot, an array argument is `<declared size>`
   slots (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) — and `return_width` is likewise how
   many slots the return value occupies (1 for a scalar return, the
   declared size for an array return). Neither is the same as counting
   *values*: a call with one array-typed and one scalar parameter has an
   arity of `<array size> + 1`, not 2.

   CALLING CONVENTION. A call expression's arguments compile left to
   right onto the stack, in whatever shape each one needs: a scalar
   argument is one ordinary expression (one value pushed), while an
   array-typed argument (matched against the parameter it fills) is a
   sequence of `<declared size>` LOAD_LOCALs copying that argument array's
   slots on top of the stack, one by one, in order (GRAMMAR.bnf design
   note 3e) — passing is always by VALUE, a fresh copy, never a reference
   to the caller's own slots. Either way, by the time CALL executes, the
   callee's entire argument list — `arity` slots' worth, whatever mix of
   scalars and array copies produced them — is simply sitting on top of
   the stack in order. CALL then:

     1. Checks the frame stack isn't already at capacity
        (CallStackOverflow if it is — this is what turns unbounded
        recursion into a catchable runtime error instead of a native
        stack overflow/crash).
     2. Pushes a frame recording the CALLER's chunk, ip (already advanced
        past the CALL instruction, so execution resumes right after it),
        base pointer, and return_width — everything needed to resume the
        caller once the callee returns, including how many slots the
        CALLER's own eventual RET will need to copy (so a nested call's
        RET can't clobber that number for the outer call still waiting on
        the stack, e.g. `sum(doubleAll(nums))`).
     3. Sets the new base pointer to `sp - arity`: since the callee's
        arguments already occupy the top `arity` slots of the stack, this
        makes them exactly the callee's LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL slots
        0..arity-1 without copying them anywhere else — an array-typed
        parameter's slots are simply a contiguous sub-range of that same
        span, addressed via LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX like any other
        array-typed local (section 5).
     4. Switches to the callee's chunk, jumps to instruction 0, and sets
        the active return_width to the callee's own (used by the RET that
        eventually returns from THIS call, not the caller's).

   RET generalizes "pop the result, restore the frame, push the result
   back" from exactly one value to `return_width` of them: a `return`
   statement's expression already leaves exactly `return_width` values
   sitting on top of the callee's stack region by the time RET executes
   (one PUSH_CONST/expression for a scalar return, `<declared size>`
   LOAD_LOCALs for an array return, mirroring how array arguments are
   copied at the call site). RET then copies those top `return_width`
   slots down onto the frame's own base pointer IN PLACE (slot-by-slot,
   `stack[bp+i] = stack[bp_relative_top+i]`) rather than through a pop/push
   pair, sets the stack pointer to `bp + return_width` (discarding every
   argument, parameter-slot, and local the callee ever pushed in one step —
   whether or not the compiler's own block-scope POP bookkeeping ran,
   since a `return` can fire from arbitrarily deep inside nested
   if/while/block bodies and always unwinds the whole frame directly),
   pops the current frame, and restores the chunk/ip/base pointer/
   return_width from it. For a scalar return (`return_width = 1`) this is
   exactly the old pop-then-push behavior, just written as a single-slot
   copy instead.

   RECURSION FALLS OUT FOR FREE. Nothing about CALL or RET is aware of
   whether the callee is the same function as the caller. Each call gets
   its own frame and its own base pointer into a fresh region of the
   *same* stack array, so a function calling itself (or two functions
   calling each other) just works: fib(n-1) and fib(n-2) each get their
   own base pointer, so their respective slot-0 values (their own `n`)
   never collide, no matter how deep the recursion goes — until either the
   frame stack (CallStackOverflow) or the value stack itself
   (StackOverflow) runs out of room.

   TOP LEVEL IS FRAME 0. There's no special-casing anywhere for "not
   inside a function": top-level code is simply the main chunk, executing
   with base pointer 0 for the program's entire lifetime, and it never
   needs a frame pushed for itself because nothing ever CALLs into it —
   it's where `run` starts. This is also why `return` is restricted to
   inside a function body (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3c) rather than a
   supported way to end the program early: RET always pops a frame that a
   CALL pushed, and no CALL ever runs before the main chunk starts, so
   there is no frame to pop at the top level.

   GENERIC ARRAYS (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3j) DON'T CHANGE ANY OF THE
   ABOVE — they just change what a "value" is at one particular slot. A
   generic (`int[]`) parameter costs exactly 1 slot for arity purposes,
   same as a scalar, because at runtime it holds exactly one `Value`: an
   ARRAY_REF (section 2). Where that reference comes FROM depends on what
   the caller passed:
     - a FIXED-size local (an array or a fixed-size parameter): the
       compiler emits MAKE_ARRAY_REF at the call site, computing
       `base = bp + <that local's slot>` using the CALLER's own bp (still
       in scope — this runs before CALL switches frames) and pushing the
       resulting reference as this one argument slot.
     - a GENERIC local (already holding a reference): LOAD_LOCAL simply
       copies the existing `ArrayRef` value onward, unchanged — its `base`
       still points wherever it always did, since a reference's target
       never moves.
   Either way, by the time CALL executes, that argument is just one more
   ordinary slot among the callee's `arity` — CALL/RET (above) don't know
   or care that a slot holds a reference rather than an INT.

   WHY A REFERENCE IS SAFE AS AN ARGUMENT BUT NOT ALWAYS SAFE AS A RETURN.
   An argument's reference is only ever *read* during the callee's own
   call, which is always fully nested inside (and therefore shorter-lived
   than) whichever frame the reference points into — so it can never go
   stale before the callee is done with it. A RETURN is different: the
   reference has to remain valid AFTER this function's own frame is
   discarded. A reference synthesized by THIS call's own MAKE_ARRAY_REF
   points into THIS frame, which RET is about to pop — returning one would
   hand the caller a reference to slots that are about to be reused by
   whatever gets called next. Only a reference this function received
   as-is (a generic parameter, or a nested call's own generic return,
   forwarded transitively) is safe to return, because by induction it was
   always ultimately synthesized as some ANCESTOR frame's call argument —
   and an ancestor frame, by definition, outlives every frame nested
   inside it. The compiler enforces this statically
   (`compiler.zig`'s `compileGenericArrayReturn`,
   `CompileError.EscapingArrayReference`) rather than leaving it as a
   runtime footgun — no ARRAY_REF a running program ever holds can be
   dangling, by construction, the same "checked at compile time, not
   trusted at runtime" spirit as bounds-checking (section 3's
   LOAD_INDEX/LOAD_INDEX_REF).


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. THE FOR-LOOP (desugars to section 4's while-stmt pattern)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `for v in start..end body` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3g) introduces its
   own wrapper scope holding two hidden locals the compiler generates —
   the range's end bound (evaluated once) and the loop variable itself —
   then emits *exactly* the while-stmt pattern from section 4, just with
   the condition/increment hardcoded to those two locals instead of
   arbitrary compiled expressions:

       <end-expr>                    (* hidden local: end_slot *)
       <start-expr>                  (* hidden local: var_slot, = v *)
     loop_start:
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       LOAD_LOCAL  end_slot
       LT
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>                        (* v resolves as an ordinary local *)
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       PUSH_CONST  #1
       ADD
       STORE_LOCAL  var_slot
       POP
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP
       (* both hidden locals popped as the wrapper scope closes,
          section 5 *)

   No new opcodes are involved — the same way 'and'/'or' (section 4) are
   sugar over JUMP_IF_FALSE/JUMP rather than dedicated instructions, `for`
   is sugar over the exact same LOAD_LOCAL/ADD/STORE_LOCAL/JUMP primitives
   every other loop already uses. Because `end_slot`'s value is computed
   once up front and never touched again, re-assigning whatever expression
   produced `end` inside the loop body has no effect on how many
   iterations run (see the "bounds evaluated once" behavior this
   guarantees). The hidden end-bound local is given the empty string as
   its name — never a legal IDENTIFIER lexeme (GRAMMAR.bnf section 1), so
   it can never collide with, shadow, or be resolved by any name a user
   actually writes.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. IMPORT/EXPORT (compile-time only - no new opcodes, no runtime
      concept of a "module")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   import/export (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) add precisely one new
   compile-time concern - which names a given file may call - and touch
   nothing at runtime: the VM has no notion of files or modules, CALL's
   operand is still a single flat index into one program-wide function
   table, exactly as it was before a program could span more than one
   file (section 6). Everything described here lives in src/module.zig and
   the multi-module entry point compiler.Compiler.compileModules
   (compileProgram is now a thin wrapper that hands compileModules a
   single module with an empty import list, so a plain one-file program's
   behavior - every function visible to every other, export irrelevant -
   is unchanged).

   THE MODULE LOADER RUNS BEFORE COMPILATION, NOT DURING IT. Given an
   entry program, module.Loader recursively lexes and parses every file
   it transitively imports, depth-first, BEFORE any compilation begins.
   Each file is keyed by a lexically normalized path (resolved relative to
   the *importing* file's own directory, GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) and
   memoized in a hash map: the second time any import path resolves to a
   key already in that map, the loader returns the already-parsed Module
   immediately instead of re-reading/re-lexing/re-parsing the file. This
   memoization is the entire mechanism behind "a shared dependency is
   compiled exactly once" - by the time compileModules ever sees the
   module list, a diamond-shaped import graph has already been collapsed
   down to one entry per distinct file, appearing exactly once in
   Loader.order. compileModules does not itself deduplicate anything; it
   simply never gets asked to compile the same module twice.

   A cycle (file A importing file B importing file A, however indirectly)
   is caught during this same loading pass - the loader keeps a stack of
   paths currently mid-load, and a path re-encountered while still on that
   stack (not yet fully loaded, so not yet in the memoization map either)
   is LoadError.CircularImport rather than infinite recursion.

   FUNCTION REGISTRATION IS GLOBAL, VISIBILITY IS PER-MODULE.
   compileModules's first pass walks every module's function declarations
   (in whatever order `modules` lists them - order doesn't matter here
   since this pass only builds a name table, the same as single-file
   compileProgram always has) and registers each one with its owning
   module's index and `exported` flag alongside the name/arity/call-index
   every function has always had. A name collision anywhere in this whole
   table - even between two files that don't import each other at all -
   is SemanticError.DuplicateFunction, the same error a single file's own
   two `func f()`s already were (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h: this is what
   keeps CALL's resolution a flat table rather than needing per-module
   namespacing).

   Compiling each module's function *bodies* (and the entry module's
   top-level statements) tracks which module is "current" and which other
   modules' indices are directly reachable from it (that module's own
   `imports` list). A call to name `f` resolves in two steps: first find
   `f` in the global table (as always - SemanticError.UndefinedFunction if
   no such name exists anywhere), then check it's *visible* from here -
   either it belongs to the current module (always allowed, exported or
   not: a module's own private helpers are always usable by itself), or
   it belongs to a module in the current module's direct-import list AND
   is exported (SemanticError.FunctionNotVisible otherwise). This is
   deliberately NOT transitive: importing B does not make B's own
   imports' exports visible here (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) - there is
   no automatic re-export.

   A BUNDLED STANDARD LIBRARY IS RESOLVED BY NAME, NOT BY PATH. Before an
   `import` path is resolved against the importing file's directory
   (section 8's opening paragraph, GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h), it's
   checked against a small table of reserved `.std.butter` names -
   currently just `"math.std.butter"` (src/stdlib.zig) - whose Butter
   source is embedded into the `butter` binary itself at build time via
   `@embedFile`. A hit resolves to that embedded source directly, keyed by
   the bare reserved name rather than a filesystem path, so it imports
   identically regardless of which directory or how many different files
   import it (still collapsed to one loaded/compiled copy either way, by
   the same memoization this section already describes) - and with no
   file named `math.std.butter` needing to exist anywhere on disk. Past
   that one lookup, a bundled module is loaded and compiled exactly like
   any other: its functions still need `export` to be visible outside it,
   it's still one graph node in `Loader.order`, and its own `import`s (if
   it had any) would go through this same by-name-first check.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   9. BYTE-STREAM I/O (three instructions, no new value kind, still no heap)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `read`/`write` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3k) add three instructions —
   READ, WRITE, WRITE_BYTES (section 3) — and, on their own, nothing else:
   no allocator, no runtime stream table of their own (files get one —
   section 10, which also explains the STREAM value these three now
   consume).

   THE STREAM IS A VALUE ON THE STACK, NOT AN OPERAND. This is a change
   from these three instructions' original design, where each carried
   which of stdin/stdout/stderr it acted on as its own u32 operand — valid
   only because a stream used to be nothing but a bare compile-time
   keyword. Files (section 10) changed that: `open`'s result is inherently
   a runtime value, and it has to be able to flow into READ/WRITE/
   WRITE_BYTES exactly like a literal `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` does. So
   all three now take ZERO operands and instead pop the STREAM they act on
   off the top of the stack — pushed there by whatever expression named
   it, a PUSH_CONST of a literal stream or the result of an OPEN — rather
   than being told which stream via their operand. This is what lets a
   file's stream reach `read`/`write` without needing three more
   instructions just for "the stream happens to be a file" instead of
   "the stream happens to be one of the three keywords": by the time READ
   executes, it doesn't need to know or care which kind of STREAM it was
   handed.

   DIRECTION IS CHECKED AT COMPILE TIME WHEN IT CAN BE, AT RUN TIME
   OTHERWISE. `compiler.zig`'s `compileStreamOperand` statically checks
   direction (`CompileError.WrongStreamDirection`) only when the stream
   expression is a literal `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` keyword use — the one
   case where which stream is meant, and therefore its direction, is truly
   known at compile time. For any other stream expression (a variable, an
   `open(...)` call, a parameter, ...) the compiler has no way to know, so
   it emits the instruction unconditionally and the VM checks instead:
   `readerFor`/`writerFor` (section 10) raise
   `RuntimeError.StreamNotReadable`/`StreamNotWritable` if the stream
   they're handed turns out to be opened in the wrong direction.

   THE BUFFER ARRIVES AS AN ARRAY_REF ON THE STACK. READ and WRITE_BYTES
   don't take a packed (slot, length) operand the way LOAD_INDEX does —
   now that their old operand is spent on nothing (the stream moved to the
   stack, above), they simply take no operand at all. Instead the compiler
   puts the buffer on the stack as a single ARRAY_REF first, reusing the
   exact instructions a generic call argument already used (section 6's
   generic-array addendum): MAKE_ARRAY_REF for a fixed-size local,
   LOAD_LOCAL for a local that already holds a reference. That one decision
   is what keeps this to three instructions instead of six — there is no
   READ/READ_REF pair, because by the time READ executes it can no longer
   tell which kind of array it was handed, and doesn't need to.

   A fresh reference synthesized for an I/O buffer is trivially safe in a
   way a returned one isn't (section 6's "why a reference is safe as an
   argument but not always safe as a return"): it is created and fully
   consumed by a single instruction inside the very frame it points into, so
   there is no window in which the frame could have been popped.

   BYTES AND VALUES ARE TWO DIFFERENT TRANSLATIONS. A Butter buffer holds
   one `Value` per byte, so both directions need a translation step through
   `Vm.io_buffer`, a fixed `[stack_max]u8` staging area — large enough by
   construction to hold any buffer the value stack itself could contain, so
   it never needs to grow and the VM stays allocator-free at run time
   (section 2):
     - READ reads into `io_buffer`, then scatters each byte out as an
       `INT` (`Vm.scatterBytes`).
     - WRITE_BYTES gathers each slot back down into `io_buffer`, checking
       as it goes that every element really names a byte
       (`Vm.gatherBytes`), then writes that flat slice once.
   WRITE (the value form) needs neither, since it is writing a rendering
   rather than bytes: it counts the rendered length with a discarding pass
   and then renders again to the real stream, so the count it pushes is
   exact for a value of any size without a staging buffer to overflow.

   EVERYTHING OUTSIDE THE VM IS INJECTED, AND MOST OF IT IS OPTIONAL.
   `Vm.run` takes a `Host { out, err, in, fs }` (section 10 covers `fs`,
   added once files needed a fourth field — `Host` was called `Streams`
   before that) rather than the single writer it took before I/O existed.
   `out` is required (PRINT has nowhere else to go); `in`, `err`, and `fs`
   are each optional, and an absent one behaves exactly like the shell
   redirection or permission it stands in for — a missing `in` is
   `< /dev/null` (every READ immediately reports 0 rather than failing), a
   missing `err` is `2>/dev/null` (bytes are counted as written, then
   dropped), and a missing `fs` is running with no filesystem access at all
   (every OPEN fails with `RuntimeError.FilesUnavailable` — section 10).
   This is what lets every test drive real I/O against in-memory buffers
   and (for files) a temporary directory, and it's why nothing in the VM
   ever reaches for a process-wide handle itself.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   10. FILES (two instructions, one new runtime table, still no heap)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `open`/`close` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3l) add two instructions — OPEN
   and CLOSE (section 3) — and one new piece of VM state: a fixed-size
   table of open files (`Vm.files`, `max_open_files = 8` slots of
   `?OpenFile`). Nothing else about the "no heap, no allocator at run time"
   goal (section 1) changes: the table is part of the `Vm` struct itself,
   the same as the value stack, so it costs no allocation to have and none
   to use.

   A STREAM NAMES A TABLE SLOT, NEVER A RAW OS HANDLE. `Value.stream`'s
   `.file` payload (section 2) is a `u32` INDEX into `Vm.files`, not an
   `std.Io.File` or file descriptor copied directly into the value. This
   indirection is what keeps a stream small, trivially copyable, and free
   of ownership semantics: copying a `Value.stream` (assigning it to
   another variable, passing it as an argument) copies only which slot it
   names, never the underlying OS resource, so there is no way for a
   Butter program to observe two "independent" copies of the same open
   file behaving differently, and no destructor/ownership-tracking
   machinery is needed anywhere a `Value` is copied. The real resource —
   an `std.Io.File`, a buffered reader OR writer (`OpenFile.Handle`, never
   both: a file is opened in exactly one direction), and its own byte
   buffer — lives entirely in the table entry, owned by the `Vm`, not by
   any `Value` a program holds. A `null` slot is a free one; CLOSE (below)
   sets a slot back to `null` unconditionally, which is what turns "using a
   closed file" into a clean `RuntimeError.StreamClosed` rather than a
   stale handle.

   OPEN #mode   ( path -- stream )    pops path (must be STRING —
                                       TypeMismatch otherwise) and opens it
                                       against `Host.fs` in the given
                                       `mode` (`value.OpenMode` — the one
                                       operand this instruction carries,
                                       since the mode is always a bare
                                       keyword and so is fixed at compile
                                       time even though the resulting
                                       stream isn't). On success, records
                                       the opened file in the first free
                                       table slot and pushes a STREAM
                                       naming it.
                                         - `RuntimeError.FilesUnavailable`
                                           if `Host.fs` is null (the
                                           embedder granted no filesystem
                                           access at all).
                                         - `RuntimeError.TooManyOpenFiles`
                                           if every table slot is already
                                           in use — a hard limit, since
                                           there is no heap to grow the
                                           table with.
                                         - `RuntimeError.FileOpenFailed`
                                           otherwise, if the underlying
                                           open/create call — or, in
                                           `append` mode, the follow-up
                                           stat/seek to the file's current
                                           end — fails (no such file in
                                           `read` mode, no permission, it's
                                           a directory, and so on).
                                       All three set `Vm.diagnostic`
                                       (`operation = "open"`, `path`, and a
                                       `cause` that is `@errorName` of the
                                       underlying error for
                                       `FileOpenFailed`, or a fixed
                                       explanatory string for the other
                                       two). `write` mode creates the file,
                                       truncating it first if it already
                                       exists; `read` mode requires it to
                                       already exist; `append` mode opens
                                       it for writing and then seeks to its
                                       current end (via `file.stat`) rather
                                       than truncating, creating it first
                                       if it's genuinely absent — that seek
                                       is the one extra step distinguishing
                                       `append` from `write`.

   CLOSE                    ( stream -- )   pops a STREAM (TypeMismatch if
                                       the popped value isn't one).
                                         - A `.standard` stream (a literal
                                           `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr`) is
                                           `RuntimeError.
                                           CannotCloseStandardStream` —
                                           those three belong to whoever
                                           started the program. The
                                           compiler already rejects this
                                           case at compile time when it can
                                           tell statically
                                           (`CompileError.
                                           CannotCloseStandardStream`,
                                           GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3l);
                                           this is the run-time backstop
                                           for when it can't (the stream
                                           came from a variable, an `if`,
                                           and so on).
                                         - A `.file` naming a slot that
                                           isn't currently open (already
                                           closed, or never a valid slot —
                                           e.g. an index beyond the
                                           table's size) is
                                           `RuntimeError.StreamClosed`.
                                         - Otherwise: flushes the file if
                                           it was opened for writing
                                           (`RuntimeError.
                                           FileCloseFailed` if the flush
                                           fails, `Vm.diagnostic` carrying
                                           `@errorName` of the cause with
                                           an empty path — there's no path
                                           left to report once a file is
                                           already open, only the stream
                                           naming it), closes the
                                           underlying OS file, and frees
                                           the table slot UNCONDITIONALLY
                                           either way — even a failed flush
                                           still frees the slot, so a close
                                           that fails can never leave the
                                           table stuck holding a handle the
                                           program has no way to get rid
                                           of.

   READ/WRITE/WRITE_BYTES (section 9) ALREADY WORK ON A FILE STREAM
   UNCHANGED — they pop a STREAM and dispatch through `Vm.readerFor`/
   `writerFor`, which handle a `.file` stream by looking up its table entry
   (`Vm.fileEntry`) and returning whichever half of its `Handle` exists:
     - reading a `.file` opened in `write`/`append` mode (or writing to one
       opened `read`) is `RuntimeError.StreamNotReadable`/
       `StreamNotWritable` — the run-time backstop for the direction check
       the compiler can only make statically for a literal `stdin`/
       `stdout`/`stderr` (section 9).
     - a `.file` naming a closed/invalid slot is `RuntimeError.
       StreamClosed`, exactly as for CLOSE above.

   EVERY OPEN FILE IS CLOSED ON THE WAY OUT OF `run`, WHETHER IT HALTED
   NORMALLY OR FAILED. `Vm.closeAllFiles` (flushing and closing every
   still-open table slot, the same as CLOSE) runs from an `errdefer` set up
   at the very top of `run` — so any propagated `RuntimeError` still closes
   every file first — AND explicitly on the ordinary HALT path, so a
   failure flushing the program's OWN files on ordinary exit is reported as
   this run's own result rather than silently discarded the way an
   `errdefer`-only close would be. A program that never `close`s a file it
   opened does not leak it for the life of the process — only for the life
   of this one `run` call — but IS still bounded by `max_open_files` while
   it runs, since the table has no allocator to grow with (section 1's
   "no heap" goal, same as the value stack).

   `Vm.diagnostic` IS HOW A RUNTIME ERROR CARRIES DETAIL A ZIG ERROR VALUE
   CAN'T. Four of the eight file-related `RuntimeError`s set it —
   `FileOpenFailed`, `TooManyOpenFiles`, and `FilesUnavailable` (all three
   from OPEN, since only `open` has a path worth reporting), plus
   `FileCloseFailed` (from CLOSE, path left empty). The other four
   (`StreamNotReadable`, `StreamNotWritable`, `StreamClosed`,
   `CannotCloseStandardStream`) carry no diagnostic — the bare error tag
   already says everything there is to say about them, with no path or
   underlying-cause string to add. `path` is borrowed straight from the
   chunk's own constant pool (never separately allocated, consistent with
   every other STRING in this VM — section 2). `main.zig` prefers this
   diagnostic when printing a runtime error to the user, falling back to
   the bare `@errorName(err)` for every `RuntimeError` — file-related or
   not — that doesn't set one.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   11. MAPS, LISTS, AND THE HEAP (the VM's first, and only, heap-managed
       value; nine of the ten new opcodes below — JSON_PARSE, the tenth,
       is section 12's)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `map`/`list` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3m) add one new runtime value kind
   — OBJECT (section 2) — and, with it, the one thing every prior section
   of this document was careful to say the VM didn't need: a real heap and
   a real allocator. `Vm` now carries a `std.mem.Allocator`
   (`Vm.init(allocator)`, a breaking signature change from the old
   zero-argument `Vm.init()`); a program that never touches a map, a list,
   or `json(...)` never calls into it.

   `value.Object` IS A REFCOUNTED HEAP HEADER, NOT A TRACED ONE.
   ```
   Object = struct {
       refcount: usize = 1,
       payload: union(enum) {
           string: []const u8,                       // heap-owned bytes
           list: std.ArrayList(Value),
           map: std.StringArrayHashMapUnmanaged(Value),
       },
   }
   ```
   `refcount` starts at 1 (the reference returned by whatever created it)
   and is adjusted by `Value.incref`/`Value.decref` (below); reaching 0
   frees the object, recursively decreffing every child `Value` it holds
   (each list element, each map value) and freeing every map key it owns —
   a plain recursive free, not a mark/sweep collector. A self-referential
   structure (`m["self"] := m`) never reaches refcount 0 and leaks for the
   life of the `Vm.run` call that built it; this is a known, accepted
   limitation (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3m), not a bug this VM tries to
   catch — `print`ing one is separately guarded by a hard recursion-depth
   cap (64), so walking a cycle to render it fails cheaply instead of
   overflowing the native stack.

   ONE `object` TAG, NOT THREE. A heap-owned STRING (e.g. a JSON string
   leaf) shares the same `Object`/refcount machinery as a list/map rather
   than being its own `Value` variant — every incref/decref site only ever
   has to ask "is this an OBJECT?" once. `Value.asStringBytes` is what lets
   a heap string and the ordinary borrowed `.string` variant (section 2)
   compare equal and substitute for each other by content anywhere a STRING
   is expected; `Value.eql` uses it for exactly that. A list/map, by
   contrast, compares by POINTER IDENTITY ONLY, never deep — no other
   `<value>` does deep comparison either (ARRAY_REF/STREAM compare cheap
   tuples), and a deep comparison could hang on the accepted cycle above.

   A MAP IS ORDER-PRESERVING, NOT A PLAIN HASH TABLE. Its entries iterate
   in insertion order (`std.StringArrayHashMapUnmanaged`, not a plain
   `HashMap`) so `keys(...)`, `print`, and a document's own JSON structure
   all see deterministic output. Every map KEY is a fresh, map-owned copy
   (`allocator.dupe`'d on first insertion, freed on removal/overwrite/
   destroy) — never a borrowed slice into whatever expression produced the
   key — which is what lets `MAP_KEYS` (below) hand back independent,
   freshly-owned values with no lifetime tie back to the map they came
   from.

   `Local.collection` IS A SEPARATE AXIS FROM `Local.array`. The compiler's
   per-local bookkeeping (section 5) gains a second, independent optional
   field — null for anything that isn't a map/list, `.map`/`.list`
   otherwise — checked BEFORE `Local.array` wherever a bare name is
   resolved. It's kept separate rather than folded into `ArraySpec` because
   `map`/`list` are new `<type>` entries that never take the `[N]`/`[]`
   array suffix `ArraySpec` represents at all; they're always exactly one
   heap-reference slot, addressed via INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET below rather than
   any array opcode. Whatever `Local.collection` says at compile time, the
   VM still re-checks the actual runtime tag before every INDEX_GET/
   INDEX_SET/LIST_PUSH/MAP_HAS/MAP_DELETE/MAP_KEYS/LEN_VALUE (`TypeMismatch`
   if it doesn't match) — the same "declared type isn't checked against
   what's actually stored" latitude every other variable already has
   (section 1), applied here too.

   BRACKET INDEXING IS GENERIC, NOT SLOT-BASED, BECAUSE IT HAS TO CHAIN.
   Unlike LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX (fixed array) and LOAD_INDEX_REF/
   STORE_INDEX_REF/LOAD_REF_LEN (generic array), which all take a
   compile-time-known SLOT as their operand, INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET take NO
   slot operand at all — they pop their container off the stack as an
   ordinary `Value`, however it got there. This is what a chained
   `doc["a"]["b"]` needs: the base `doc["a"]` is a value already sitting on
   the stack, not a named local, so a slot-taking opcode literally couldn't
   express it. The compiler's dispatch (`compileIndex`/`compileIndexAssign`)
   reflects this directly: a bare identifier resolving to a FIXED/GENERIC
   array local still goes through the existing slot-based opcodes
   unchanged, with no chaining possible (an array element is always
   scalar, GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e, so there is structurally no way to
   reach this case with a non-identifier base); a bare identifier resolving
   to a `.map`/`.list` local, or ANY non-identifier base at all (a nested
   index, a call, a literal), compiles `base` as an ordinary expression
   (LOAD_LOCAL, or recursively through another INDEX_GET) and falls through
   to INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET. Indexing into a value that turns out to be
   scalar at runtime — including one produced by chaining off a fixed-array
   element — is `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch`, not a special chaining-depth
   check; this is how "a fixed/generic array element is never itself
   indexable" stays enforced without the compiler needing to reason about
   chains at all. A bare identifier resolving to neither an array nor a
   `.map`/`.list` local is instead a COMPILE-time `SemanticError.
   NotIndexable` — the one case the compiler can already rule out
   statically.

   REFCOUNTING: INCREF ON DUPLICATION, DECREF ON DISCARD-WITHOUT-MOVING.
   `Value.incref`/`Value.decref` are no-ops for every non-OBJECT value,
   which is what keeps them cheap to call unconditionally. The general
   rule: incref happens exactly when a `Value` is duplicated into a second
   live location while the first stays live; decref happens exactly when a
   live location's `Value` is permanently destroyed or overwritten without
   moving. A pure MOVE (temporarily on the expression stack, then relocated
   to a new permanent home with nothing left behind referencing it) needs
   neither — this covers every var-decl initializer, MAKE_LIST/MAKE_MAP's
   element transplant, LIST_PUSH's appended value, and RET's copied-down
   return value. Concretely:
     - LOAD_LOCAL, LOAD_INDEX, LOAD_INDEX_REF: the pushed copy is increffed
       (an array CAN hold a heap-backed `.string` element even though it
       can never hold a `.map`/`.list` one, so these needed updating too,
       not just the new opcodes).
     - STORE_LOCAL, STORE_INDEX, STORE_INDEX_REF: the new value is
       increffed BEFORE the old slot value is decreffed — in that order,
       so `x := x` (old and new the same object) can never free the only
       live copy before the incref meant to preserve it runs — then the old
       value is decreffed.
     - POP: decrefs the popped value. PRINT and WRITE (section 9) likewise
       decref the value they render before discarding it — printing/
       writing a list/map/heap-string is a real possibility now, and both
       are otherwise exactly this same "read then discard" shape as POP.
     - EQ/NEQ (section 3) decref BOTH operands after comparing them — the
       one case where a non-numeric pair (e.g. two lists, by identity)
       reaches a SUCCESSFUL comparison rather than `TypeMismatch`, so
       there's a decref to do on the ordinary success path, not only on an
       error path.
     - ADD/SUB/MUL/DIV/MOD/POW/comparison (via `popNumericPair`) and
       NEG/NOT decref their operand(s) on the `TypeMismatch` path (a
       non-numeric/non-boolean operand might be a heap object) — the
       success path never needs to, since succeeding requires the operands
       to be non-refcounted scalars in the first place.
     - RET decrefs every value in `stack[bp .. sp-return_width)` — the
       departing frame's own locals/arguments/temporaries — BEFORE the
       existing copy-down loop moves the return value(s) into place. Safe
       even though this range can overlap the copy destination: the decref
       pass only ever READS a slot strictly below `sp-return_width`, and
       the copy loop only ever WRITES into `[bp, bp+return_width)`, and
       `bp <= sp-return_width` always — so nothing is ever double-decreffed
       or read after being overwritten.
     - `Vm.run`'s two exit paths (the `errdefer` for any propagated error,
       and the ordinary HALT path) each decref every value still in
       `stack[0..sp)` in one pass, generalizing `closeAllFiles`'s own
       exit-path cleanup (section 10) to the heap — needed because
       top-level code (frame 0) never runs a RET of its own, so without
       this its own locals would never get decreffed at all.
   No change is needed for PUSH_CONST (a compile-time constant is never an
   OBJECT), MAKE_ARRAY_REF/LOAD_REF_LEN (an ARRAY_REF is never refcounted),
   or CALL (it copies nothing — every argument was already increffed by
   whatever expression produced it).

   NEW OPCODES: see section 3 (MAKE_LIST, MAKE_MAP, INDEX_GET, INDEX_SET,
   LIST_PUSH, MAP_HAS, MAP_DELETE, MAP_KEYS, LEN_VALUE) for exact stack
   effects; JSON_PARSE is covered in section 12.

   A MAP/LIST VALUE NEEDS NONE OF THE GENERIC-ARRAY-REFERENCE ESCAPE
   ANALYSIS SECTION 6 DESCRIBES. `compileGenericArrayReturn`'s soundness
   rule (a reference synthesized from THIS frame's own locals can't be
   returned, only one already received as a parameter) exists because an
   ARRAY_REF points into the VM's STACK, which the returning frame is about
   to reuse. An OBJECT reference points into the HEAP instead, with its own
   refcounted lifetime completely independent of any stack frame — so a
   map/list can be returned from a function, stored inside another map/
   list, or handed to a function and returned back, with no restriction at
   all. `compileCallCommon`'s ordinary argument-compiling path already
   handles a map/list-typed parameter correctly with no changes: since
   `param.array_size` is always null for one, it falls through to the same
   `compileExpr(arg)` any scalar argument already used, and a map/list
   return likewise falls through to the ordinary (non-array) `return`
   compilation path — in both cases what's being passed around is simply
   one more ordinary `Value` that happens to be an OBJECT reference.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   12. JSON (one opcode, no new heap concept beyond section 11's)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `json(buffer, count)` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3n) adds one instruction —
   JSON_PARSE (section 3) — and, on its own, nothing else: no new value
   kind (a parsed document is built entirely out of section 11's ordinary
   OBJECT/NULL/scalar values) and no new VM state.

   THE BUFFER ARRIVES EXACTLY LIKE READ/WRITE_BYTES'S DOES. JSON_PARSE
   gathers its `count` bytes via the SAME `Vm.gatherBytes`/`io_buffer`
   staging area WRITE_BYTES already uses (section 9), reusing that
   mechanism unchanged rather than inventing a second way to flatten a
   Butter buffer into a flat `[]u8` — exactly the same reuse `emitArrayRef`
   already gets between generic call arguments and I/O buffers (section 6).

   PARSING ITSELF IS `std.json`'S JOB, NOT A HAND-WRITTEN ONE. `src/json.zig`
   parses the gathered bytes via `std.json.parseFromSlice` into a
   `std.json.Value` tree (in a throwaway scratch arena, freed immediately
   after conversion — entirely separate from, and never sharing storage
   with, the Butter heap objects the conversion below builds), then walks
   it: a JSON object becomes a map, a JSON array becomes a list, a JSON
   string becomes a fresh heap-owned `Object{.string}`, a JSON number
   becomes an INT or FLOAT (matching whichever `std.json` produced), a JSON
   boolean becomes BOOL, and JSON `null` becomes NULL. Every heap object
   the conversion builds already has the refcount (1) a fresh owner
   expects, exactly as if it had been built by ordinary MAKE_LIST/MAKE_MAP/
   `mapSet` calls — JSON_PARSE itself needs no incref for the value it
   pushes.

   `json` IS A SPECIAL FORM, NOT AN ORDINARY CALL, BECAUSE ITS RESULT TYPE
   ISN'T KNOWABLE UNTIL THE BYTES ARE READ. A `func`'s return type is fixed
   and declared once (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3c); a parsed document's root
   could be any `<value>` kind depending on what the bytes actually say.
   `json` is parsed like `len`/`read`/`write`/`open` — a keyword the parser
   recognizes directly in `<atom>` position — precisely so it can hand back
   a value whose kind isn't fixed at compile time, something an ordinary
   CALL (section 6), with its one fixed `return_width`, has no way to do.

   MALFORMED INPUT IS `RuntimeError.JsonParseFailed`, diagnosed via
   `Vm.diagnostic` (`operation = "json"`, `path` left empty, `cause` a
   fixed description) — the same side-channel files established (section
   10) for an error whose bare tag can't carry enough detail on its own.
   Unlike `FileOpenFailed`'s `cause` (the underlying OS error's own name),
   JSON's `cause` is a fixed string rather than `@errorName` of some
   specific underlying error: `src/json.zig` collapses every parse failure
   (a syntax error, a truncated document, and so on) to one
   `error.JsonParseFailed` before it ever reaches the VM, so there's no
   more specific name left to report by the time `Vm.diagnostic` is set.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   13. JSON STRINGIFY (one opcode, section 12's reverse — still no new heap
       concept, still no new VM state)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `stringify(value)` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3o) adds one instruction —
   JSON_STRINGIFY (section 3) — the reverse of JSON_PARSE: instead of
   consuming bytes to build a value, it consumes a value to build bytes,
   handing them back as a fresh heap `Object{.string}` (section 11) rather
   than through the buffer convention READ/WRITE_BYTES and JSON_PARSE share,
   since there's no destination buffer here for it to fill.

   THE ENCODER IS A RECURSIVE WALK OVER `Value`, THE MIRROR IMAGE OF
   `convert`'s WALK OVER `std.json.Value` (section 12). `src/json.zig`'s
   `stringify` walks `v` directly — INT/FLOAT print as JSON numbers, BOOL as
   `true`/`false`, NULL as `null`, and OBJECT's `.list`/`.map` payloads
   recurse with `[...]`/`{"k":v,...}` punctuation — needing no intermediate
   `std.json.Value` tree the way `parse` needs one on the way in, since
   JSON_STRINGIFY only ever produces text, never Butter heap objects, as it
   goes.

   STRING ESCAPING IS `std.json`'S JOB, NOT A HAND-WRITTEN ONE — THE SAME
   SPLIT `parse` ALREADY MAKES. Both `Value.string` (borrowed) and
   `Object{.string}` (heap) leaves go through `std.json.Stringify.
   encodeJsonString`, which handles quote/backslash/control-character
   escaping (and surrounds the result in quotes) exactly as any other
   `std.json` consumer gets it, rather than JSON_STRINGIFY reimplementing
   that escaping table itself.

   NOT THE SAME RENDERING `PRINT` USES, ON PURPOSE. `Value.print` (section
   2) already renders a list/map in JSON-*shaped* syntax for human-readable
   output, but writes a string element as bare `"{s}"` with no escaping at
   all — correct only because `print`'s own job is producing text for a
   person to read, never text meant to be re-parsed. JSON_STRINGIFY exists
   specifically to produce text a `json(...)` call elsewhere can read back,
   so it cannot share `print`'s implementation; `src/json.zig`'s `write`
   helper is a second, independent renderer that happens to agree with
   `print` on every value kind's *shape* while differing on every string
   leaf's *content*.

   A STREAM OR ARRAY REFERENCE IS `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch`, THE SAME
   BARE-TAG CONVENTION MAP_HAS/LIST_PUSH ALREADY USE FOR A WRONG-KIND
   OPERAND (section 11) — no `Vm.diagnostic`, since "value has no JSON
   representation" is everything there is to say. `src/json.zig` reports
   this to the VM as `error.Unstringifiable`, collapsed to `TypeMismatch` at
   the JSON_STRINGIFY handler exactly as `error.JsonParseFailed` collapses
   to `JsonParseFailed` at JSON_PARSE's.

   `stringify` IS A SPECIAL FORM FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN `json` IS.
   `json`'s result type isn't knowable until its bytes are read (section 12)
   — that's what rules out an ordinary `func`. `stringify`'s result type
   is always `string`, exactly as fixed as any `func`'s declared return
   type (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3c); what rules out an ordinary `func`
   here instead is that nothing in this language lets a `func` body build a
   `string` a piece at a time — no concatenation, no formatting primitive —
   the way JSON_STRINGIFY's own recursive walk needs. So it's parsed like
   `json`/`len`/`read`/`write`/`open` regardless — a keyword `<atom>`
   recognizes directly, not a CALL.
   ========================================================================== *)
